  </section>

  <section class="trust">
    <div class="trust__container">
      <div class="trust__items">
        <div class="trust__item">
          <span class="trust__text">300 Piece MOQ</span>
        </div>
        <div class="trust__item">
          <span class="trust__text">Transparent FOB Pricing</span>
        </div>
        <div class="trust__item">
          <span class="trust__text">Manufacturing Since 1998</span>
        </div>
        <div class="trust__item">
          <span class="trust__text">GOTS Certified</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section__container">
      <h2 class="section__title">The Real Answer: It Depends on Your Model, Not Your Ambition</h2>
      <p class="section__text">
        Search "how much to start a clothing brand" and you'll find answers ranging from $500 to $50,000, and both are technically true — they're just describing different businesses. The number that matters isn't how big your dream is, it's which of three models you're actually building: print-on-demand with zero inventory risk, a custom cut-and-sew brand holding your own stock, or a wholesale-ready label that needs retail-grade inventory from day one. Each has a genuinely different cost floor.
      </p>
      <p class="section__text">
        <strong>Print-on-demand or blank-based, $2,000-5,000.</strong> You're not committing to bulk inventory — products get made per order, or you're customizing pre-made blanks. This is the cheapest way to test whether a design actually sells before you risk real capital, but per-unit cost is the highest of the three models and you have limited control over fabric, fit, and finish.
      </p>
      <p class="section__text">
        <strong>Custom cut-and-sew, direct-to-consumer, $10,000-30,000.</strong> This is where most serious first-time brands land. You own the pattern, choose your fabric, and run a real production order — typically 2-4 styles at 300 pieces each is achievable inside this range once you add a website, basic branding, and initial marketing. This is the model our <a href="/low-moq-clothing-manufacturers/" style="color:#e00;text-decoration:none;">low MOQ manufacturing</a> is built around.
      </p>
      <p class="section__text">
        <strong>Wholesale-focused, $25,000-50,000+.</strong> If you're targeting retail buyers or a showroom from launch, minimum order quantities jump, you need retail-ready packaging and compliance documentation, and cash gets tied up in inventory for longer before it converts to revenue. This tier isn't really a "startup" budget so much as a "first-year operating" budget.
      </p>
      <p class="section__text">
        Production is the largest line item in every model — it's the one cost you can't skip, unlike marketing polish or a custom website. Our <a href="/tools/moq-calculator/" style="color:#e00;text-decoration:none;">MOQ calculator</a> gives you a real number for your specific styles rather than a generic range.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

