  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="section__container">
      <h2 class="section__title">Production: The Cost You Can't Skip</h2>
      <p class="section__text">
        Every other line item on this page is negotiable — you can DIY your branding, delay paid marketing, launch on a free website theme. Production isn't optional, and it's usually 40-60% of what you actually spend before your first sale. At our 300-piece minimum, a basic cotton t-shirt runs $3-8 FOB per piece depending on fabric weight and print complexity, so a single style at minimum quantity costs roughly $900-2,400 before freight and duty. Add 20-40% on top of the FOB price for shipping, insurance, and customs to get a realistic landed cost — that's the number that should actually go in your budget, not the factory quote alone.
      </p>
      <p class="section__text">
        Two decisions move this number more than anything else: how many styles you launch with, and what MOQ your factory requires. Three styles at a 300-piece MOQ is a very different budget than three styles at a 1,000-piece MOQ most larger factories default to — the difference is often the gap between a $3,000 first order and a $10,000 one, for the exact same designs. If you want the full breakdown by garment type, fabric cost, and volume tier, our <a href="/clothing-manufacturing-cost-guide" style="color:#e00;text-decoration:none;">clothing manufacturing cost guide</a> covers that in depth — this page focuses on where production fits into your total startup budget, not the per-unit mechanics.
      </p>
      <p class="section__text">
        Standard payment terms in Bangladesh manufacturing are 30% deposit to start production, 70% before shipment — worth knowing when you're planning cash flow, since that deposit is due before you've sold anything. Our <a href="/tools/price-calculator/" style="color:#e00;text-decoration:none;">price calculator</a> gives you a specific number for your actual designs rather than a category average.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section__container">
      <h2 class="section__title">Marketing: The Cost Most Founders Underbudget</h2>
      <p class="section__text">
        Here's the pattern we see most often with first-time brands: nearly the entire budget goes to production, samples, and a nice-looking website, and marketing gets whatever's left over — usually a few hundred dollars. That's backwards. A reasonable rule of thumb is to plan on spending at least as much on marketing in your first year as you spent on product development. Fashion e-commerce customer acquisition cost typically runs $25-50 per customer even with a mostly-organic strategy, so if you're aiming for your first 100 customers, that's realistically $2,500-5,000 in marketing spend on top of whatever comes from word of mouth.
      </p>
      <p class="section__text">
        Organic content — product photos, short-form video, consistent posting — costs time more than money, and it's where nearly every brand should start regardless of budget tier. It also produces the raw material (photos, testimonials, footage) that makes paid ads actually convert once you're ready to spend on them. Trying to run paid ads before you have any organic proof of demand is one of the more expensive mistakes new brands make.
      </p>
      <p class="section__text">
        Micro-influencer partnerships (roughly 10K-100K followers) tend to offer the best cost-to-reach ratio for a new brand — many will work for product instead of cash on a first collaboration, which keeps your cash marketing spend close to zero while still generating content and reach. Save paid influencer budgets for partners who've already proven they convert for you.
      </p>
      <p class="section__text">
        Email remains the highest-ROI channel available once you have any traffic at all — even a basic welcome sequence and launch announcement through a platform like Klaviyo (roughly $30-150/month at startup volume) captures sales you'd otherwise lose. Set it up before launch, not after.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="section__container">
      <h2 class="section__title">Website and E-commerce: Where Not to Overspend</h2>
      <p class="section__text">
        Shopify's Basic plan is $39/month if you pay monthly, or $29/month if you commit to annual billing — either way, it's the entry point for a real storefront and it's genuinely sufficient for a launch. That covers transaction processing (2.9% + 30¢ per online sale), unlimited products, and 24/7 support. Founders sometimes skip straight to a custom-built site before they've validated demand, which is money better spent on your first production run.
      </p>
      <p class="section__text">
        Beyond the monthly fee, budget for a premium theme ($150-350 one-time — free themes work but rarely match your brand's visual identity), and a small set of essential apps for email marketing and reviews, which usually run $50-100/month combined at startup scale. Product photography is the other real cost: a first shoot with a freelancer typically runs $200-800, or you can shoot it yourself with a smartphone and natural light if the budget genuinely isn't there.
      </p>
      <p class="section__text">
        Put together, a professional-looking store live on Shopify Basic with a premium theme and decent photography lands around $500-2,000 to set up, plus the ongoing $39/month (or less annually) and app fees. Upgrade to Shopify's Grow plan ($105/month) once you're consistently doing enough volume that its lower transaction fees actually pay for the difference — for most new brands, that's a problem to have later, not a decision to make on day one.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section__container">
      <h2 class="section__title">Branding: Where DIY Actually Works</h2>
      <p class="section__text">
        This is the category where cutting corners hurts you the least — a genuinely well-designed logo from a freelancer on Fiverr or Upwork ($50-200) looks just as professional in a photo as one from an agency charging $2,000, and most customers will never know the difference. Reserve the bigger budget for production and marketing instead.
      </p>
      <p class="section__text">
        A basic brand kit — logo, a defined color palette, one or two fonts, and simple usage guidelines — runs $200-1,000 depending on whether you DIY it with free templates or hire a freelancer to put it together properly. Packaging is a separate decision: a simple logo sticker on plain mailers costs almost nothing and works fine at launch; custom printed packaging ($500-1,000 for a first order) is worth it once unboxing is actually part of your brand experience, not before.
      </p>
      <p class="section__text">
        Photography deserves more of the budget than logo design does — it's what customers actually see before they buy. A single photoshoot with a freelance photographer for your first collection typically runs $200-800 and produces content you'll reuse across your website, social media, and ads for months.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="section__container">
      <h2 class="section__title">Legal: Small Now, Expensive to Fix Later</h2>
      <p class="section__text">
        Legal costs are small relative to production or marketing, but skipping them is the mistake that gets expensive later rather than at launch. Business formation — an LLC in most US states — runs $100-500 depending on the state, and it's worth doing before you take your first sale rather than after, since it separates your personal assets from business liability from day one.
      </p>
      <p class="section__text">
        Trademark registration with the USPTO costs $225-400 per class (clothing brands typically file under Class 25), and it's genuinely worth doing early — the alternative is discovering a year in that your brand name is already trademarked by someone else, after you've built recognition around it. DIY filing is possible but complex enough that many founders use an attorney for anything beyond the most basic filing.
      </p>
      <p class="section__text">
        Terms of service, a privacy policy, and a clear refund policy round out the essentials — template services like Rocket Lawyer or a similar provider cover this for $200-1,000, and it's worth having an attorney review anything custom, like a wholesale agreement, before you sign it. Basic liability insurance ($300-1,000/year) is also worth budgeting once you're shipping product, since it's the one cost on this page that protects everything else you've spent money on.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
